<section>
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div class="pic"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1764232288442-f80108db48b2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="durian fruits hanging from a tree branch" width="800" height="672" loading="lazy"></div>
    <div>
      <span class="tag">Durian etiquette</span>
      <h2>Enjoying durian considerately</h2>
      <p>Durian&#8217;s aroma travels, so a little planning keeps everyone happy, including people nearby who may not love it as much as you do.</p>
      <ul class="chk"><li>Open durian outdoors, away from crowded spots.</li><li>Carry pods in an airtight box, double-bagged.</li><li>Bring gloves or a cloth to handle the spiky husk.</li><li>Take all shells and seeds home for disposal.</li><li>Rinse hands with water and a little salt to reduce the smell.</li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap faq"><span class="tag">Questions</span><h2 style="margin-bottom:20px">Durian picnic FAQ</h2><details open><summary>Why does durian smell so strong?</summary><p>Ripe durian releases a mix of natural sulphur compounds and fruity esters. Some people find the aroma sweet and custardy; others find it overpowering. The smell is strongest in very ripe fruit.</p></details><details><summary>Is it okay to take durian on a picnic?</summary><p>Yes, as long as you&#8217;re thoughtful. Choose an open, uncrowded spot, bring the fruit in a sealed container and take all shells home with you. Many hotels and public transport systems in Southeast Asia don&#8217;t allow durian, so check local rules first.</p></details><details><summary>How do I know if a durian is ripe?</summary><p>A ripe durian has a noticeable aroma near the stem, feels heavy for its size and may have small cracks starting along the seams. The stem should look fresh rather than dried out.</p></details><details><summary>How long does opened durian keep?</summary><p>Store the flesh in an airtight container in the fridge and eat it within two to three days. You can also freeze pods for up to three months; they&#8217;re delicious half-frozen.</p></details><details><summary>What goes well with durian?</summary><p>Durian pairs beautifully with sticky rice, coconut cream and pandan. Juicy, refreshing fruit like mangosteen or watermelon makes a lovely contrast, as durian itself is very rich and filling.</p></details></div></section>

